# Bowling Game Kata

```elixir
Mix.install([{:bowling_game, path: Path.expand("..", __DIR__)}])
```

## The rules in one minute

A game has 10 frames. Each frame has up to two rolls to knock down 10 pins.

* **Strike (X)**: all 10 pins on the first roll. Scores 10 plus the next **two** rolls.
* **Spare (/)**: all 10 pins in two rolls. Scores 10 plus the next **one** roll.
* **Open frame**: scores the pins knocked down.
* **10th frame**: a strike or spare earns bonus balls (max 21 rolls in a game).

## Rolling

`roll/2` validates every roll and returns `{:ok, game}` or `{:error, reason}`.
Use `roll!/2` when you would rather raise.

```elixir
game = BowlingGame.new()

{:ok, game} = BowlingGame.roll(game, 7)
{:ok, game} = BowlingGame.roll(game, 3)

BowlingGame.roll(game, 11)
```

```elixir
# Only 4 pins are standing after a 6, so a 5 is impossible.
game = BowlingGame.roll!(BowlingGame.new(), 6)

{BowlingGame.pins_standing(game), BowlingGame.roll(game, 5)}
```

## Scoring as you go

A strike or spare waits for its bonus rolls, so the score only grows once they
are known. An unfinished game never crashes.

```elixir
rolls = [10, 7, 3, 9, 0]

for count <- 1..length(rolls) do
  game = Enum.reduce(Enum.take(rolls, count), BowlingGame.new(), &BowlingGame.roll!(&2, &1))
  {Enum.take(rolls, count), BowlingGame.score(game)}
end
```

## The score card

```elixir
game = Enum.reduce([10, 7, 3, 9, 0], BowlingGame.new(), &BowlingGame.roll!(&2, &1))

game |> BowlingGame.Scoreboard.render() |> IO.puts()
```

`frames/1` gives the same information as data:

```elixir
BowlingGame.frames(game)
```

## A perfect game

```elixir
perfect = Enum.reduce(1..12, BowlingGame.new(), fn _, g -> BowlingGame.roll!(g, 10) end)

IO.puts(BowlingGame.Scoreboard.render(perfect))
{BowlingGame.score(perfect), BowlingGame.game_over?(perfect)}
```

## Play it yourself

Change the list below and re-evaluate the cell.

```elixir
my_rolls = [10, 10, 5, 4, 8, 2, 7, 1]

my_game = Enum.reduce(my_rolls, BowlingGame.new(), &BowlingGame.roll!(&2, &1))

IO.puts(BowlingGame.Scoreboard.render(my_game))
{BowlingGame.score(my_game), BowlingGame.pins_standing(my_game)}
```
